In this lesson we'll look at comparing circumferences of different circles. Imagine walking once around the very edge of a circle. That total distance you cover is called the circumference. Think of it as the circle's version of a perimeter. Now here's the cool pattern. If you roll a circle along a straight line and mark how far it travels in one full spin, you'll notice something. A circle with a diameter of ten centimetres travels about thirty-one point four centimetres. Bump that diameter up to one metre and it travels about three point one four metres. Crank it way up to a hundred metres and you get about three hundred and fourteen metres. Every single time, the circumference divided by the diameter gives the same number, roughly three point one four one five nine, and we call that number pi. The reason it's always the same is that all circles are similar, just scaled-up or scaled-down versions of each other, so the ratio is a universal constant. By the way, when you need an exact answer, keep pi in your working rather than rounding it early. Now let's see it in action.